WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.760
Film Courage: How many submissions has Hollyshorts
received each of the past five years?

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.080
Daniel Sol, Co-Founder of the Hollyshorts
Film Festival: So five years ago we were at

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.400
around 2,000 or so submissions for that year.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.661
This past year we received 5,000 or so submissions
so in-between that we’ve gone from 2,000

00:00:16.661 --> 00:00:21.380
to 5,000 for the last 5 years increased around
500 or so for each of those years.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:26.619
So 2,000, 2,500, 3,000, 3,500, so on and so
forth.

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:29.660
To now last year we’re at 5,000 submissions
for the 14th year.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.870
This is our 15th year coming up and we’re
expecting potentially 6,000 submissions.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:38.130
That’s a big increase and we might jump
up a thousand this year based on averages.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:43.061
Of course, early-bird deadline is coming up
next Friday so we’re estimating where it

00:00:43.061 --> 00:00:47.770
could be so we’ve seen a big jump these
last five years definitely.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.379
Film Courage: Very cool.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:55.559
Of those multi-thousand plus films that you’ve
received since the inception of Hollyshorts

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:58.290
how many would you say you’ve personally
watched?

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.790
Daniel: A lot.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:06.369
I say it used to be 100 %, now it’s not
as possible with amount of films and a lot

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:09.280
of family and new duties and things on top
of that.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.520
It’s probably a good at least 80% of all
films.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:13.520
Film Courage: Wow.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.310
Daniel: If not even more.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.810
The last few years we’ve grown a review
team where I’m overseeing the process more

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:24.649
now and watching certain scores and rewatching
some films and watching my own but I’m not

00:01:24.649 --> 00:01:27.759
just hands down watching everything like it
was before say five years ago where I was

00:01:27.759 --> 00:01:30.249
literally hunkered down watching every single
film.

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:35.009
2,000 films is much easier than watching five
to six thousands naturally just time permitting

00:01:35.009 --> 00:01:36.009
right?

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:42.240
So I’d say that’s probably a healthy and
safe number because I like to see everything.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.149
As much as I can possibly do.

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:48.189
It’s a personal sense of pride and also
maybe I am too controlling?

00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:49.189
I don’t know.

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:51.539
Film Courage: Well your name is on it so sure.

00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:52.539
Daniel: I’m not sure I just want to watch
all the films.

00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:57.420
So there’s that part of it and in the end
films that I am watching or other people are

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.820
watching and reviewing the scores, I’m going
to program the films.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.009
I program the festival.

00:02:01.009 --> 00:02:03.959
So in the end the films that do get in I’m
watching all of them.

00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:07.439
So of that number you asked if you look at
the number of the films that are accepted

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:10.069
or in the festival those I watch 100% of.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:15.349
So naturally yes I’m going to review scores
of other judges and say okay I want to watch

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:17.769
these, these and these and I’m going to
make sure and let’s see what they are talking

00:02:17.769 --> 00:02:21.469
about here and kind of just double check their
work and if I like the films then I can sort

00:02:21.469 --> 00:02:26.820
of make that sort of passive approval I’ll
be the one to program the film so I’m sort

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.170
of the last go around if it gets in or not
kind of thing.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.900
Film Courage: I’m curious about this review
team.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:37.579
How are they and how do you screen them to
know they are really indie film fans.

00:02:37.579 --> 00:02:42.209
Daniel: Well we do a process of giving the
a soft start right?

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:46.293
Okay hey this is our criteria, this is what
we look for, we have a very big breakdown,

00:02:46.293 --> 00:02:49.829
very thorough breakdown of how we judge, what
we score, what we’re looking for, what the

00:02:49.829 --> 00:02:55.489
process is, understanding how many films come
in because if you just watch films for for

00:02:55.489 --> 00:02:56.489
the film.

00:02:56.489 --> 00:03:00.010
If you’re just watching a film, you might
like a film and you think it’s okay and

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.299
there’s elements you like and there are
elements you don’t like but you’re like

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:06.719
okay this has a chance, you might score a
little lenient or something not understanding

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:11.469
that there are 6,000 submissions and there
are only 400 spots that actually can get in

00:03:11.469 --> 00:03:12.900
so that number is what?

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.609
5 to 8% can actually make the festival, it’s
very competitive.

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:20.049
So if you’re looking at it from that standpoint
and only watch five to ten films they may

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:24.299
not understand the full scope of how many
films are there, does that make sense?

00:03:24.299 --> 00:03:25.939
There’s a lot of films to watch.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:28.900
So if they’re just looking at their batch,
they may not understand the full scope so

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.739
we try to break down a very thorough listed
out email of what we’re looking for, what

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:38.079
the scope of how many films we receive, what
kind of films we have received, looking at

00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:41.109
the line-ups from the years past (we share
the line-ups), hey look at these, review our

00:03:41.109 --> 00:03:48.159
schedule so you understand what we’ve show,
the genres, styles different things so they

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:52.310
understand when they are looking at the films
okay what is the full scope of how the festival

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.939
programs and what we’ve had before and what
we’re looking for and things like that.

00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:00.519
So we give them all that and again assign
only a few films to start if we love their

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:04.420
results and then feel it’s on par with what
we’re looking for then we’ll keep them

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.180
on board to review many more films and oversee
the process.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:14.380
If it is too…it’s usually too lenient
is more troubling for me personally honestly.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:20.670
It is a very competitive festival and we don’t
want someone who every film is just obviously

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.349
“home run and it’s amazing.”

00:04:22.349 --> 00:04:23.770
And you watch the film and it’s not on par.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:25.630
That’s not what we’re looking for.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:29.660
Now at the same time if you are too harsh…you
know something could be a fantastic film and

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:34.009
they’re grading it like a three out of ten
and it’s like “Okay, you’re being unreasonable.”

00:04:34.009 --> 00:04:37.289
So there are both sides and you don’t want
that.

00:04:37.289 --> 00:04:40.230
If that’s the case, sometimes we let them
know “Hey, we’re just moving on.”

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:45.550
Or some people we just quite honestly move
on and don’t respond anymore, like we’re

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:49.250
going in another direction we just look for
other people and things like that.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.720
But yes, that’s basically the process.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.680
If you like what there early work is with
a few films we can keep them on for more.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.820
Film Courage: Of the people that you know
in the film industry, is there anyone who

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.750
has watched as many short films as you have?

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.470
Daniel: I don’t know?

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.470
I would say probably no.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:09.930
I think at this time now we talked about this
years ago I would say “Who knows?

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:10.930
I don’t know.”

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:14.000
I think it’s safe to say when you look at
the math of all the films over all this many

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.180
years and since we’re essentially just shorts
only and not features, I think we’ve probably

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:24.110
watched as many shorts as anyone in the world
I guess?

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:25.669
It’s a lot of movies we’ve watched!

00:05:25.669 --> 00:05:29.910
So I start thinking like “Yes, have I watched
more shorts than anyone?”

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:34.150
I don’t know it sounds weird to say but
it’s possible.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.810
It’s a lot of films.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:45.720
I mean you’re talking at least…I don’t
know say on 15 years averages 2,500 films

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:46.720
say?

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.919
What is that number?

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:51.690
30,000 movies or something?

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:55.919
I don’t know, it’s a lot of movies so
we’ve seen a lot of shorts.

